# Chapter 4 · Women as Candidates: Getting onto the Ballot

> The syllabus names "**gender issues in women as candidates**". The 2024 question asked "what obstacles are faced by women politicians in Pakistan in pursuing a career in politics?", and the 2018 question asked whether we need equal numbers of women in politics.

## 4.1  The candidate stage — where women drop out

Voting is a right every adult can use; standing for office requires **resources, networks and a party ticket**. This is where most women drop out of politics. Political scientists describe the path to office as a **pipeline** with three filters:

1. **Eligibility** — who could stand (all adult citizens meeting legal conditions).
2. **Aspiration** — who wants to stand.
3. **Selection** — whom parties choose, and whom voters elect.

::: fig flow | The candidate pipeline: where women leak out.
@per 4
Eligible citizens: half are women
Aspirants: fewer women want to stand — socialisation, family duties, risk
Party tickets: gatekeepers choose men for "winnable" seats
Winners: 11 women won general NA seats in 2024
:::

::: data Women candidates, 2024 general election | ECP / IFES
**306 women** contested general seats for the National Assembly; **11 won** — 5 independents, 4 from PML-N and 2 from PPP. In ==2018== **8** women won general seats; in ==2013== **5** won at the general election (10 including by-elections).
:::

## 4.2  The legal framework

- Any citizen aged **25** or over who is on the electoral roll may contest a National Assembly or provincial seat (Article 62 sets further conditions).
- From ==2002== to ==2008== candidates had to hold a **graduate (BA) degree** — a rule that excluded many rural women and men, and was dropped in ==2008==.
- The **Elections Act 2017**, **Section 206**, requires every party to give at least **5%** of its tickets for general seats to **women**.

::: law Elections Act 2017 | Section 206 — women's tickets
A political party shall, while making the selection of candidates for elected bodies, ensure at least **five per cent** representation of **women candidates** on general seats.
-> A **legislated candidate quota**, the first in Pakistan's history. But it is weak: it does not say *which* seats, so parties often give women tickets in constituencies they expect to **lose**.
:::

## 4.3  Barriers to women's candidacy

::: fig tree | The barriers facing women candidates in Pakistan.
Barriers to women candidates
- Party barriers
  - Male gatekeepers choose candidates
  - Women given "unwinnable" seats
  - Reserved seats seen as women's "place"
- Money and patronage
  - Campaigns cost tens of millions of rupees
  - Women own little land or capital
  - Patronage networks are male
- Social barriers
  - Norms of purdah and honour
  - Family consent needed
  - Care work and mobility limits
- Violence and harassment
  - Threats, character attacks, online abuse
  - Campaigning at night and in public spaces
- Electoral system
  - FPTP favours "electables" with clan support
  - Biradari and feudal networks
:::

### (1) Party gatekeeping

Political parties decide tickets, and their leaderships are overwhelmingly male. Parties prefer "**electables**" — candidates with landholdings, clan (*biradari*) support and money — who are mostly men. Many party leaders also see **reserved seats** as the proper route for women, so they see no need to give women general tickets.

### (2) Money

Campaigns for a National Assembly seat cost huge sums — for posters, rallies, transport on polling day and payments to local power brokers. Women own little land or capital and have less access to business donors. Election spending limits are often ignored.

### (3) Social norms and family

Campaigning means travelling, meeting male voters, attending gatherings at night and appearing in public — all difficult where **purdah** and family **honour** restrict women's mobility. Women need family **consent**, and many must balance politics with **care work**.

### (4) Violence and harassment

Women candidates face **character assassination**, threats, and increasingly **online abuse**. In some areas women have faced threats from militants. Fear of violence deters women from standing at all.

### (5) The electoral system

Under **first-past-the-post**, one candidate wins each constituency. Parties then pick the "safest" candidate — usually a man with local networks. **Proportional representation** with list quotas, as Nepal uses, tends to elect more women.

### (6) Dynasties — door-opener and limit

Many women who win general seats come from **political families** — daughters, wives or widows of politicians. Dynasties open doors for some women, such as **Benazir Bhutto** (daughter of Zulfikar Ali Bhutto) and **Maryam Nawaz** (daughter of Nawaz Sharif). But they keep politics closed to women without such connections.

::: balance | Are dynastic women "real" politicians?
- **Critics:** dynastic women are proxies for male relatives; their success says nothing about ordinary women's chances.
- **Defenders:** many dynastic women became strong leaders in their own right — Benazir Bhutto led a party and twice became prime minister; dynasties are common among male politicians too.
-> **Line to take:** dynasty is a **route**, not a disqualification; the problem is that it is almost the **only** route for women.
:::

## 4.3a  Electoral systems and women — FPTP versus proportional representation

The type of electoral system is one of the strongest predictors of how many women are elected.

::: table | How electoral systems affect women's election.
@widths 3.2,5.4,5.4
Aspect | First-past-the-post (FPTP) | Proportional representation (PR)
How it works | One winner per constituency — the candidate with the most votes | Seats shared by parties according to vote share, usually from lists
Party's incentive | Pick the single "safest" candidate — usually a man with local networks | Present a balanced list that appeals to all voters
Effect on women | Women rarely chosen in winnable seats | Easier to include women; quotas can require alternating ("zipper") lists
Examples | Pakistan's 266 general seats; India; UK | Nordic countries; Nepal's PR seats; Pakistan's reserved seats
:::

Pakistan uses **both**: FPTP for its 266 general seats, where only **11** women won in ==2024==, and a PR-like allocation for its **60** reserved seats. The contrast is striking — women are almost absent where parties must choose one candidate, and guaranteed only where seats are allocated from lists. **Nepal**'s mixed system shows the same pattern: women won about **35%** of all seats in ==2026== but only **14** of the FPTP seats.

Reform options that keep direct election but help women include **dual-member constituencies** (each with one woman), **rotating reserved constituencies** (a different set reserved for women at each election, as in India's panchayats), or a stronger **candidate quota** requiring parties to field women in seats they won or came close to winning last time. Each would give women a constituency and voters of their own, rather than dependence on a party list.

## 4.4  Why fewer women aspire to stand

Even before parties choose, fewer women **want** to stand. Research in many countries shows that women are less often **encouraged** to run, more likely to doubt their qualifications, and more concerned about the effect on their families. In Pakistan, **socialisation** — the message that politics is "dirty" and a man's world — reinforces this. Encouragement from party leaders, mentors and women's networks raises women's political ambition.

## 4.5  Cases of women candidates

::: case Benazir Bhutto (1953–2007) | the first woman prime minister of a Muslim-majority country
Chairperson of the PPP after her father's execution, she led the party to victory in ==1988== and became Pakistan's (and the Muslim world's) first woman prime minister (MCQ 2018); she served again from ==1993== to ==1996==. She was assassinated in Rawalpindi on ==27 December 2007== while campaigning.
-> Shows both the possibility of women's leadership and the violence women politicians can face.
:::

::: case Women winners on general seats, 2024 | breaking through
Eleven women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024==, the highest number in years. They included party leaders and dynastic candidates as well as independents backed by PTI. Yet 11 of 266 general seats is only about **4%**.
:::

## 4.5a  Women candidates in local government

Local councils are where most Pakistani women have actually stood for office. Under the **Local Government Ordinance 2001**, **33%** of seats were reserved for women and filled by direct or indirect election; about **36,000** women were elected in ==2000–01==. In conservative areas, some seats went **unfilled** because no women were allowed to file papers, and some winners were proxies for male relatives. But many women gained confidence, public experience and respect, and some later won provincial seats. Under the provincial local government laws of the 2010s, women's seats continued but at lower shares in most provinces. Local quotas are the **training ground** that a strong candidate pipeline needs.

## 4.5b  Online harassment — a new barrier

Social media has become a main campaign tool, and with it has come a new barrier. Women candidates and legislators face **trolling**, **doctored images**, sexual slurs and threats online — attacks on their character rather than their policies. The **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act** (==2016==) criminalises some of this, and the **Digital Rights Foundation** helpline (==2016==) supports victims, but enforcement is weak. Research in many countries shows that online abuse leads some women to withdraw from politics or never enter it.

## 4.6  Policy: getting more women on the ballot

1. **Strengthen Section 206** — raise the quota (many activists propose **10–33%**) and require tickets in **winnable** seats, for example by counting only seats the party won or came second in last time.
2. **Sanctions** — refuse election symbols or public funds to parties that fail to comply.
3. **Campaign finance** — enforce spending limits; give public funds or free airtime to women candidates.
4. **Party reform** — quotas for women in party executives and ticket committees.
5. **Safety** — act against violence and online harassment of women candidates.
6. **Pathways** — use **local government** seats as a training ground, then move women to provincial and national tickets.

::: eye | Using this chapter
For "obstacles faced by women politicians" (2024), organise barriers as **party, money, social, violence, system**, and give the data: **306 contested, 11 won** (2024); **5%** tickets under Section 206.
:::
